ass: spare a run the layers its clip hides whole

No image is made of a bitmap that lies outside the clip rectangle, but by
then the run has been combined and blurred and its shadow copied and
shifted. That is paid for every frame by text that has left the screen
under a \move, and by an effect that draws a line as its shadow alone
(fill and border at alpha FE) and throws the shadow off the screen with
\t(\yshad-4500): a second later nothing is left to see, and for the three
seconds the event still lasts the shadow's place in the key makes every
frame a new composite of nineteen lines of text.

Before a run's composite is looked up, its glyphs' bitmaps are now held
against the clip rectangle as render_glyph applies it, widened by what \be
and the blur can add around them (ass_blur_reach: the widest kernel at the
blur's level, an upper bound). A run that is outside with all of that gets
no composite. One whose shadow alone is outside gets none of that, and so
a key without it: fill and border are the same bitmaps with a shadow and
without. The opaque box of BorderStyle 3, which is drawn as its own shadow,
and \iclip are left alone. The images of a frame are the same. That
episode, one thread on a Fire TV Stick 4K Max, with this patch and the
one before it: 54.1 s of rendering instead of 60.4 s, 172 frames later
than their duration instead of 272, the longest 464 ms instead of 799 ms.

scripts/test_libass_clipped_layers.c: what a blur brings into a clip from
a box beside it is the open clip's image, cut, for every blur and
distance; a line whose shadow is off the screen is the line without one;
a line off the screen still casts its shadow onto it.

